#X text 20 20 u_map -- THE MAP \, and the only file in Cut It that says what a control MEANS. Every device below publishes named controls onto param and stops there \; everything above is driven by the buses this file writes. Nothing in e_ or c_ may ever learn that a nanoKONTROL exists \, and this is the layer that keeps that true., f 58;
#X text 20 165 creation args: none. inlets: none. outlet 1: THE VOLCA \, selector-prefixed -- notes <n> <v> <d> \, cc <c> <v> \, program <n>. One outlet per OUTPUT DEVICE \, wired in u_root \, because no bus carries a sounding note., f 58;
#X text 700 20 THE MAP IS A TABLE NOW \, AND IT IS MODE-DEPENDENT. cut-it-map.txt sits beside this file \, one row per mapping \, FOUR ATOMS ALWAYS: <mode> <control> <dest> <arg>. A control means whatever the row for the CURRENT mode says it means \, and 42 controls times six modes is far past the ten-mapping threshold at which the old one-branch-per-mapping comment said to revisit., f 76;
#X text 1400 20 ⛔ AND THE ALLOWLIST GUARD IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT MAKES IT ACCEPTABLE. The table does not name a SEND -- it names a destination that must exist as a literal argument on the route box below \, feeding a handler you can see on this canvas. There is no send with a variable name anywhere in this file. The set of things a control can reach is still the set of boxes you can READ \, which is the property the old comment existed to protect. Skip the guard and it is gone silently \, because nothing fails and no test notices., f 76;
#X text 2200 20 ⚠️ A ROW NAMING A DESTINATION THAT IS NOT ON THE ROUTE IS A REAL ERROR \, and it is the one failure this design can have that nothing else would catch. It goes to err as unknown-dest. Contrast the moses miss below \, which is an unmapped control and is NORMAL and SILENT., f 62;
#X text 20 1700 THE SIX TRANSPORT KEYS STAY HARDCODED \, AND THEY COME FIRST. They ARE the mode selector \, so a mode change can never itself be mode-dependent -- and if the table were ever empty or broken you could still change mode on a device with no console. Everything the route does not match falls out of its reject and goes to the table., f 62;
#X text 1080 1700 THE DIVISOR \, AND THE ONE THING NOBODY HAD WRITTEN DOWN: param VALUES ARE NOT ONE UNIT. nano faders and knobs are 0-127 \, nano buttons send 1 \, Launchpad pads 0 or 127 \, 404 pads a real velocity \, but MOTHER'S OWN CONTROLS ARE 0-1 -- the four Organelle knobs and aux. So one destination fed from two surfaces would need two scalings. Everything is divided by 127 EXCEPT those five \, and every handler below sees 0 to 1., f 62;
#X text 2140 1700 ⛔ THE list trim IS LOAD-BEARING AND ITS ABSENCE IS SILENT. list split emits SYMBOL og-knob-1 \, whose selector is symbol rather than the name \, and route matches a SELECTOR -- measured \, and without the trim the route NEVER matches \, every control takes the reject \, and the Organelle's 0-1 knobs are divided by 127 into nothing., f 62;
#X text 20 1930 THE REJECT IS THE NORMAL PATH HERE \, which is the opposite of the route above the handlers. Almost every control in the rig is 0-127 \; the five that are not are the exception being tested for. The t b is still the reject-outlet rule -- a reject carries the DATA that failed to match \, never a bang., f 62;
#X text 1080 1930 THE VALUE IS PARKED AND THE NAME DOES THE WORK. list split fires its REMAINDER first \, so the value is normalised and stored in list append's right inlet before the name below has even started the lookup. Then the looked-up destination arrives on the LEFT and picks the parked value back up., f 62;
#X text 2140 1930 THE LOOKUP IS TWO ATOMS WIDE. text search with a two-atom key matches leading fields 0 AND 1 with no field arguments at all -- measured \, item 229 -- so mode-1 og-knob-1 finds the row for that control IN THAT MODE. A miss returns -1 \, which leaves moses's LEFT outlet as DATA rather than a bang \, and NOTHING is connected to it: an unmapped control is the normal state of most controls and must stay silent., f 62;
#X text 20 2160 ⚠️ A DUPLICATE mode-and-control PAIR SILENTLY LOSES. text search returns only the FIRST match -- measured -- so a second row for the same pair is dead and nothing says so. The gate's static lint is what catches it \, because the patch cannot., f 62;
#X text 1080 2160 ARG THEN VALUE \, ALWAYS \, AND unpack GIVES THEM IN THE RIGHT ORDER FOR FREE. Every branch receives arg then value as two floats \, and unpack fires RIGHT TO LEFT -- so the value lands in a cold inlet or sets a gate before the arg arrives to trigger. That is the same guarantee m_volca's t b f had to be built by hand to get., f 62;
#X text 2140 2160 THE TEMPO AND TRANSPORT CHAINS ARE THE ORIGINALS \, UNCHANGED. Knob 1 still scales 0-1 over 10 to 500 BPM and rounds with + 0.5 then i \, which is what stops the footer flickering between 119.7 and 120.1. The transport still holds NO state of its own -- it reads back what actually HAPPENED from r start and r stop \, so a start from a bench cannot leave the button pressing the wrong way., f 62;
#X text 20 2390 THE SPIGOT IS THE TRIGGER TEST \, and it is the same three boxes everywhere. A nano button sends 1 \, a pad sends 127 \, an Organelle aux sends 1 and a 404 pad sends a real velocity -- all non-zero after normalising \, and a RELEASE is zero. So the gate opens on non-zero and the arg passes through it \, which is one uniform test rather than a different threshold per surface., f 62;
#X text 1080 2390 THE VOLCA HANDLERS BUILD THE SELECTOR-PREFIXED MESSAGE m_volca EXPECTS \, and list trim is what turns list notes 48 100 200 into a message whose SELECTOR is notes. Without it m_volca's route would reject every one of them straight to err -- loudly \, at least \, which is why that reject exists., f 62;
#X text 2140 2390 volca-prog USES THE VALUE AS A GATE RATHER THAN AS DATA \, because a program number is the ARG. The value only decides whether the press counts \, so a fader release cannot select a patch., f 62;
#X text 20 2620 THE TABLE IS A FILE RATHER THAN text define -k \, so it diffs one row at a time and can be edited without opening Pd. A relative read resolves against the PATCH FOLDER -- no path \, no creation argument \, no absolute path -- and it works identically on the Mac \, on the device and inside the gate's scratch copy. Item 229., f 62;
#X text 1080 2620 ⛔ read -c TAKES THE FLAG FIRST. read cut-it-map.txt -c warns and then reads the whole file as ONE line \, which is a table that matches nothing. ⛔ AND THE READ IS AT loadbang WITH NO DELAY \, WHICH IT DID NOT USED TO BE. It sat behind del 2000 to keep a missing file's error clear of deploy.sh's 735 ms output gate -- but MOTHER PUSHES knobs.txt AT BOOT \, long before 2000 ms \, so the tempo knob's restored position hit an EMPTY TABLE and was silently dropped. The instrument booted at u_tempo's own 120 instead of the saved 57 \, and nothing reported it. Item 234. A missing map SHOULD fail a deploy \, so that error is now welcome rather than something to hide from. AN EMPTY TABLE IS REPORTED for the same reason., f 62;
#X text 2140 2620 THE MODE NAME IS THE SECOND ATOM. mode carries class then name \, and the class is u_err's business rather than this file's -- so the key is mode-1 and not compose mode-1. A second r mode rather than a tap off the state chain below \, because receives are free and order-independent \, and it leaves a working chain alone., f 62;
#X text 20 2850 THE SEED FILLS A SILENCE \, IT DOES NOT SET A DEFAULT. Nothing else writes mode at load \, so without this the grid boots with no mode to show. Any incoming mode closes the spigot \, so a real selection always wins. The seed's own send closes it too \, harmlessly \, because Pd is synchronous and the bang has already passed through., f 62;
#X text 1080 2850 u_map IS THE FIRST CONTRIBUTOR TO THE STATE STORE \, and mode is an AUTO key: a running value rather than a committed take \, so it is pushed whenever it changes and u_state flushes it on its own clock. ⬜ THE TABLE ITSELF IS NOT PERSISTED \, and that is deliberate -- it is code rather than a running value \, and nothing rewrites it at runtime. Live re-assignment is v0.4 \, and it brings its own persistence with it., f 62;
#X text 2140 2850 RESTORE ARRIVES AS A REAL SELECTION. route restore then route mode leaves compose mode-1 with compose as its SELECTOR \, which is exactly the shape s mode wants -- so no list trim on this side. It lands at about 3.5s \, long after the seed at 500ms \, and the seed's spigot is already shut., f 62;
#X obj 2820 1160 unpack f f;
#X obj 2820 1230 * 127;
#X obj 2820 1280 + 0.5;
#X obj 2820 1330 i;
#X obj 2820 1390 pack f f;
#X obj 2820 1450 list prepend pad;
#X obj 2820 1510 list trim;
#X obj 2820 1620 outlet;
#X obj 2820 250 loadbang;
#X msg 2820 300 mode-1;
#X text 2820 360 ⛔ THE LOOKUP KEY GETS ITS MODE AT LOAD \, NOT FROM THE SEED. The mode bus is seeded at 500 ms \, and the table is read at load -- but MOTHER PUSHES knobs.txt BEFORE EITHER \, so the tempo knob's restored position arrived when the key had no mode at all and missed every row. Two separate races \, one symptom: the instrument booted at 120 instead of the saved 57 and said nothing. This sets the key SYNCHRONOUSLY at load so a lookup is never waiting on a clock. A real mode still overwrites it \, from the seed or from a restore. Item 234., f 62;
